WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.760
Confucius lived at the end of the Spring and Autumn period, and was a teacher.

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:06.038
He valued benevolence, courtesy, and the importance of education.

00:00:06.388 --> 00:00:09.148
He taught his students books, and also the principles of being a good person.

00:00:09.498 --> 00:00:12.978
Confucius hoped rulers would govern with benevolence and propriety.

00:00:13.328 --> 00:00:16.280
He set out with his disciples to travel to different states.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:20.518
Confucius believed good politics must begin with the ruler himself.

00:00:20.868 --> 00:00:23.316
They traveled to many places, and the road was very hard.

00:00:23.666 --> 00:00:27.218
Sometimes there was no food, yet everyone kept going.

00:00:27.568 --> 00:00:30.136
The disciples took turns pushing the cart, and no one complained.

00:00:30.486 --> 00:00:35.286
Some rulers received Confucius politely, but did not adopt his ideas.

00:00:35.636 --> 00:00:38.588
Though disappointed, Confucius never gave up teaching.

00:00:38.938 --> 00:00:42.658
Confucius said: ideals are not easy — one must still persevere.

00:00:43.008 --> 00:00:45.480
On the road, he taught his disciples wherever and whenever he could.

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:48.950
The disciples recorded their teacher's words, sentence by sentence.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:52.060
Passersby were moved by his words and gave them some food.

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:55.890
Later, the disciples' records formed the Analects.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:59.480
Confucius's thought has influenced Chinese culture for more than two thousand years.

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:03.070
His students spread everywhere, carrying his thought still farther.

